L680: ### B.5 Proof of cite129†Theorem 4 L681: ###### Proof.
L682: 
L683: Recall that the expectile $\xi_{\nu}(\theta)$ is implicitly defined as the unique solution to
L684: 
L685:  | $$\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi):=\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l_{\nu}(F-\xi)\right]=0.$$  |  | (43)
L686: 
L687: Since $\xi_{\nu}(\theta)$ satisfies $\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi_{\nu}(\theta))=0$ for all $\theta$, applying the Implicit Function Theorem under cite90†(A1) yields:
L688:  | $$\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi)\Big|_{\xi=\xi_{\nu}(\theta)}+\nabla_{\xi}\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi)\Big|_{\xi=\xi_{\nu}(\theta)}\cdot\nabla_{\theta}\xi_{\nu}(\theta)=0.$$  |  | (44)
L689: 
L690: We now compute the partial derivatives of $\mathcal{M}$ with respect to $\theta$ and $\xi$.
L691: 1. Partial derivative w.r.t. $\theta$: Let $p_{\theta}$ be the distribution induced by the policy. Given cite90†(A1) to cite91†(A4) , the gradient $\nabla_{\theta}p_{\theta}(\tau)l_{\nu}(F-\xi)$ is well-defined and bounded. Invoking the Dominated Convergence Theorem, we express the gradient of the expectation over $p_{\theta}$ as:
L692:  | $\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi)$  | $\displaystyle=\nabla_{\theta}\int p_{\theta}(\tau)l_{\nu}(F-\xi)d\tau$  |
L693:  |  | $\displaystyle=\int\nabla_{\theta}p_{\theta}(\tau)l_{\nu}(F-\xi)d\tau$  |
L694:  |  | $\displaystyle=\int p_{\theta}(\tau)\nabla_{\theta}\log p_{\theta}(\tau)l_{\nu}(F-\xi)d\tau$  |
L695:  |  | $\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l_{\nu}(F-\xi)\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}|s_{t})\right]$  |
L696:  |  | $\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l_{\nu}(F-\xi)G(\theta)\right].$  |  | (45)
L697: 2. Partial derivative w.r.t. $\xi$: We differentiate the expectation with respect to $\xi$. Under cite97†(A2) , the discounted cost is bounded, allowing us to interchange the derivative and integral via the Dominated Convergence Theorem.
L698: 
L699:  | $\displaystyle\nabla_{\xi}\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi)$  | $\displaystyle=\nabla_{\xi}\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l_{\nu}(F-\xi)\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi}l_{\nu}(F-\xi)\right].$  |
L700: Let $u=F-\xi$. The function $l_{\nu}(u)$ is continuous and piecewise linear. Its derivative with respect to $u$ is given by $l^{\prime}_{\nu}(u)=\nu\mathbb{I}\left\{u>0\right\}+(1-\nu)\mathbb{I}\left\{u\leq 0\right\}$. Although $l_{\nu}$ is non-differentiable at $u=0$, cite130†(A3) ensures that the event $F-\xi=0$ occurs with probability zero. Thus, the derivative exists almost everywhere. Using the chain rule ($\frac{\partial u}{\partial\xi}=-1$), we get:
L701:  | $$\nabla_{\xi}\mathcal{M}(\theta,\xi)=\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l^{\prime}_{\nu}(F-\xi)\cdot(-1)\right]=-\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l^{\prime}_{\nu}(F-\xi)\right].$$  |  | (46)
L702: 
L703: 3. Solving for the gradient: Substituting (cite131†45 ) and (cite132†46 ) into (cite133†44 ):
L704: 
L705:  | $$\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l_{\nu}(F-\xi)G(\theta)\right]-\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l^{\prime}_{\nu}(F-\xi)\right]\nabla_{\theta}\xi_{\nu}(\theta)=0.$$  |  | (47)
L706: 
L707: Rearranging terms yields the desired result:
L708:  | $$\nabla_{\theta}\xi_{\nu}(\theta)=\frac{\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l_{\nu}(F-\xi_{\nu}(\theta))G(\theta)\right]}{\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[l^{\prime}_{\nu}(F-\xi_{\nu}(\theta))\right]}.$$  |  | (48)
